meta {
  name: App B reports as bob is 403
  type: http
  seq: 2
}

get {
  url: {{appB}}/api/reports
  body: none
  auth: oauth2
}

auth:oauth2 {
  grant_type: password
  access_token_url: {{tokenUrlA}}
  username: {{bobUser}}
  password: {{bobPassword}}
  client_id: {{clientAWeb}}
  client_secret: {{clientAWebSecret}}
  scope: openid profile email
  credentials_placement: body
  credentials_id: domainA-bob
  token_placement: header
  token_header_prefix: Bearer
  auto_fetch_token: true
  auto_refresh_token: true
}

assert {
  $res.status: 403
}

tests {
  test("bob is authenticated but not authorized: 403, not 401", function() {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(403);
  });

  test("Keycloak issued him a token quite happily", function() {
    // If the token request itself had failed, Bruno would not have reached the API at all and the
    // status would be 401. Getting a 403 proves the token was minted and accepted as authentic.
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.not.equal(401);
  });
}

docs {
  Bob's token is completely valid - he is a real user of SSO domain A and Keycloak issued it without
  complaint. He simply lacks the app-b-user role, so Application B refuses him.

  401 would mean "I do not know who you are". 403 means "I know exactly who you are, and no".
  Telling those two apart is the fastest way to locate a problem; see ../../../docs/troubleshooting.md.

  carol is the mirror case: she holds app-b-user but not app-a-user, so she is welcome here and
  refused by Application A. Swap the credentials above to {{carolUser}} / {{carolPassword}} (already
  in environments/local.bru) and this request turns into a 200.

  A distinct credentials_id from alice's request is essential. Sharing one would hand Bruno alice's
  cached token, and this test would "pass" as a 200 for entirely the wrong reason.
}
